    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.delete_edges_ws - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.delete_edges_ws.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.delete_edges_ws()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Delete all selected edges.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.delete_edges_ws()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Delete all selected edges.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.delete_edges_ws()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Delete all selected edges.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nDelete all selected edges.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L3425-L3541){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.delete_edges_ws()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Delete selected edges](../assets/graphs/delete-edges-ws.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: a six-node graph with edges B–C and D–E selected in the accent color. Right: those two edges are gone, shown dashed, while all six nodes and the other five edges stay in place.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nRemoves edges in the current selection from the graph. Node connectivity may be affected,\nbut nodes themselves remain in the graph. Updates selection state automatically.\n\nLike the other `_ws` (\"with selection\") methods, this acts on the current edge selection\ninstead of taking arguments. Build the selection with `select_edges()` (a predicate on\nedge objects), `select_edges_by_id()`, `select_edges_by_node()` or an edge traversal such\nas `trav_out_edge()`, then delete everything selected in one call. Common uses include:\n\n- pruning weak connections below a weight threshold\n- removing edges of a particular `rel` type\n- cutting the edges around a set of nodes while keeping the nodes themselves\n\nThe deleted edges are removed from the edge selection (which ends up empty), and an empty\nselection makes the call a no-op. Removing edges can disconnect the graph or leave\nisolated nodes behind. The graph is modified in place and, when at least one edge is\nremoved, the deletion is recorded as a single step in the step log (see `steps`). To\ndelete selected nodes together with their edges, use `delete_nodes_ws()`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Self for method chaining.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~select_edges`) \n\n:   Select edges with a predicate.\n\n[](`~delete_edge`) \n\n:   Remove one edge.\n\n[](`~delete_nodes_ws`) \n\n:   Delete the selected nodes.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThis operates on the current edge selection. Deleting the two middle edges of a\nfive-node path leaves every node in place, since removing edges never removes nodes, and\nnode \"3\" is left on its own:\n\n::: {#b73aa1f5 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(5)  # path: 1-2-3-4-5\n\nmiddle = graph.find_edge_ids(\"2\", \"3\") + graph.find_edge_ids(\"3\", \"4\")\ngraph.select_edges_by_id(middle)\ngraph.delete_edges_ws()\n\ngraph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges(), graph.get_degree(\"3\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(5, 2, 0)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Only the large payments\n\n`payments()` holds a month of payments, most of them small. Selecting the payments under\n1,000 with a predicate on their weight and deleting them leaves the large ones. Laying out\nthe whole network first, with `layout_kamada_kawai()`, keeps every account where it was\nin it:\n\n::: {#844bccfe .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npayments = ec.payments()\npayments.layout_kamada_kawai(weighted=False)\n\n\ndef is_small(edge):\n    return edge.weight < 1000\n\n\npayments.select_edges(is_small)\npayments.delete_edges_ws()\n\npayments.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n16\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEvery account is still there, now with only its large payments. Besides the wages and\nthe rent, they are the two circles of payments, Eve's payment to Finn and the chain from\nCruz to Lena, while the Grocer and the Cafe, paid only small amounts, are left on their\nown:\n\n::: {#df1fa9e6 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npayments.plot(layout=\"pos\", edge_width_by=\"weight\", label_by=\"label\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(2